-- Finds Shale's C that no program reaches, from the splitter the C recipes
-- share (cookbook/c/items/, a Roc package). GCC's unused-function warning
-- passes by every `static inline` function (each header's and those of .c
-- files a unit may not call), and a function some other dead function calls
-- is no warning at all, so this reads the whole tree's names instead:
--
--   dead       a function, or a generator (`VEC_DEFINE(Name, T)`), that no
--              program reaches: no item names it, or only items that are
--              themselves unreached do (`named only by unreached code: ...`);
--   test_only  a function outside the tests that only tests reach.
--
-- One line a finding, `path:line: dead: name` or `path:line: test_only:
-- name`, then the counts by rule and by folder.
--
--   shale cookbook:c/find_dead_code.shale            over c/
--   shale cookbook:c/find_dead_code.shale dir=c/host
--   shale cookbook:c/find_dead_code.shale self_test=1
--
-- How it reads a use. Every function, test, test list, data item, macro and
-- generator is a node; its edges are the identifiers its masked text names
-- (the splitter's `uses` for a body, the whole item's words for data, a
-- macro and a generator), so a comment, a string and a member after `.` or
-- `->` are never a use, a prototype is none, and a name taken without a
-- call (a function pointer in a table) is one. A name resolves to its own
-- file's item of that name when the file has one (two test files may each
-- have a `pick`), else to every item of that name in the tree. A generator
-- answers to its type's name and to each function its macro `makes`
-- (`VEC_DEFINE(Ints, int)` to `Ints`, `Ints_make`, `Ints_push`), so a use of
-- `Ints_push` reaches it. A macro is reached by being named, and reaches the
-- names in its body.
--
-- The programs are the roots: each `int main(` outside a `tests` folder
-- (c/shale.c, c/shale_gate.c) is production's; each test, test list and
-- `main` under one is the tests'. A function of the tests' own files (under
-- a `tests` folder, or a file named `test*`, as c/foundation/test.h, the
-- runner) that the tests reach is a test's helper, never a finding.
--
-- What it can't see: a name made by `##` outside a generator (only
-- test.h's `TEST` and `TEST_PART` do that, and tests are roots), and a
-- use that a preprocessor branch compiles out: every branch counts.
--
-- `self_test=1` runs it over cookbook/c/plants/find_dead_code/ and holds the
-- findings to the plants' own list, exactly: each way to be used (a call, a
-- pointer in a table, a macro, a generator's function or its type) and each
-- near miss (a comment, a string, a member, a prototype, recursion, a macro
-- nobody uses, a same-named helper in another test file).
--
-- It is written in Roc, the app cookbook/c/find_dead_code/find_dead_code.roc
-- and the modules beside it (Graph, the nodes, their edges and what the
-- programs reach; SelfTest, the plants' check), over the splitter package
-- cookbook/c/items/, on Shale's Roc platform (roc_platform/README.md); this
-- file is its launcher. It hands every value it is given to
-- roc_platform/run.shale in the checkout this cookbook is in (the `cookbook`
-- mount's parent), which builds the Roc there (cached; the first build after
-- a change to C Shale compiles the platform's host, 25 to 50 s) and runs the
-- program rooted here, so `dir=` is this root's path. A value the Roc reads
-- no `Shale.arg!` of stops the run before anything is built. `reads=inputs`
-- builds and runs nothing, and prints the real path of each file the program
-- is built from (run.shale's header). It is built with `opt=speed`, as
-- map_file is: the masker reads a line a byte at a time, which Roc's dev
-- build does several times slower than the Shale original's regular
-- expressions; a stop in a speed build names the platform's driver, not the
-- Roc's line. Its goldens, the Shale original's output on the plants, are
-- held by cookbook/c/find_dead_code/check_goldens.shale.
-- budget: programs=10 output_bytes=8000000

const SCRIPT = "cookbook/c/find_dead_code/find_dead_code.roc"
const CHECKOUT = dirname(dirname(real("cookbook:README.md")))
const HERE = real(".")

-- `self_test` is read here by name, as the cookbook's full tier finds the
-- recipes it runs `self_test=1` of. `arg_names()` is every value given; the
-- rest are read by a name made as the run goes, so Shale's own check cannot
-- see them: run.shale holds them to the script's reads.
const SELF_TEST = arg("self_test", "")
local values = {"script=" .. SCRIPT, "in=" .. HERE, "opt=speed"}
if SELF_TEST ~= "" then push(values, "self_test=" .. SELF_TEST) end
for name in arg_names() do
  if name ~= "self_test" then push(values, name .. "=" .. arg(name, "")) end
end
local r = run({"shale", "--root", CHECKOUT, CHECKOUT .. "/roc_platform/run.shale", "--args-stdin"}, {stdin = join(values, "\n") .. "\n", timeout_ms = 1800000, whole = 8000000})
print(trim_end(r.out))
if r.code ~= 0 then fail("find_dead_code: the Roc program stopped (exit " .. r.code .. ")") end
